WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.272
This example replaces the literal text KB with keyboard.

00:00:03.272 --> 00:00:07.152
Run the query and compare the raw description with the expanded version.

00:00:07.152 --> 00:00:11.667
This is appropriate because the transformation rule is explicit and easy to audit.

00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:19.122
In larger projects, repeated mapping rules may be better stored in reference tables rather than hard-coded

00:00:19.122 --> 00:00:19.952
into one long SQL expression.

00:00:19.952 --> 00:00:24.432
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:24.432 --> 00:00:30.027
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:30.027 --> 00:00:32.252
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:32.252 --> 00:00:37.422
In a real setting such as an operations team aligning timestamps across locations,

00:00:37.422 --> 00:00:44.122
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:44.122 --> 00:00:44.532
before discussing syntax.

00:00:44.532 --> 00:00:50.160
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
